      <p class="eyebrow">Commentary | Elders and health</p>
      <h1>Aging well starts before long-term care</h1>
      <p class="deck">A new regional long-term-care proposal has reopened a larger question: are our communities investing enough in what helps Elders remain connected to family, land, language and community before institutional care becomes necessary?</p>
      <p class="byline">By Windigo Kaan | Contributor review draft, July 26, 2026</p>

      <p>The need for more long-term-care capacity is real. More than 130 people are waiting for 32 beds in Espanola, and families across the region are often forced to look far from home for care.</p>

      <p>But a bed cannot be our whole strategy for aging.</p>

      <p class="standfirst">Long-term care may be necessary for some people. Aging well begins much earlier, in the homes, relationships and communities that help people remain well and connected for as long as possible.</p>

      <h2>The regional proposal</h2>

      <p>On June 26, Whitefish River First Nation, Sagamok Anishnawbek, the Town of Espanola and the Espanola Regional Hospital and Health Centre announced a partnership to explore a new regional long-term-care home.</p>

      <p>The partners signed a Memorandum of Understanding. The hospital says it would operate the home, Espanola has committed land near the hospital, and planning would be guided by the four partners. The announcement says community members can expect updates and opportunities for input as the project progresses.</p>

      <p>This is still an exploratory project. The public announcement does not establish final financing, a construction schedule, the number of beds reserved for either First Nation, or the complete governance and cultural-care model.</p>

      <p>Those details matter. So does the order in which decisions are made. Being told about a project is not the same as helping decide what the project should be. Consultation should shape the plan before the important choices are settled, not simply explain those choices afterward.</p>

      <h2>A different example in Wiikwemkoong</h2>

      <p>Wiikwemkoong offers a useful comparison. The Wikwemikong Nursing Home is a non-profit long-term-care home located in the community. It currently has 59 beds and has served First Nation and non-First Nation Elders for decades.</p>

      <p>The existing building no longer meets current space and design standards. Wiikwemkoong is planning a new 96-bed Elders Home and has been documenting the project through its public infrastructure updates.</p>

      <p>This does not prove that one model will answer every need. It does show that the location, ownership, governance and cultural design of long-term care are choices. A home located in the community can keep Elders closer to family, language, land and familiar relationships. That should be part of any serious comparison of options.</p>

      <h2>What the research says about aging well</h2>

      <p>Research on Indigenous aging describes wellness as more than the absence of illness. A 2022 review identified four connected themes: holistic health and wellbeing, maintaining connections, resilience and a positive attitude, and confronting systemic and structural barriers.</p>

      <p>The First Nations Information Governance Centre similarly approaches aging through strengths rather than deficits. Its work looks at the total health of the person in the total environment, including culture, community and the conditions that make wellness possible.</p>

      <p>That points toward practical investments long before a person needs a long-term-care bed:</p>

      <ul>
        <li>safe and accessible housing;</li>
        <li>home care, nursing, personal support and health navigation;</li>
        <li>reliable transportation;</li>
        <li>nutritious food and opportunities for physical activity;</li>
        <li>support for caregivers and family members;</li>
        <li>language, ceremony, spirituality and connection to the land; and</li>
        <li>places and programs that keep Elders connected to other generations.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>These supports do not eliminate every need for institutional care. They can help people remain healthier and at home longer, while improving life for Elders whether or not they eventually enter a care home.</p>

      <h2>The shortage is longstanding</h2>

      <p>A 2018 First Nations long-term-care working-group presentation identified only four long-term-care homes located in First Nation communities, with a combined 223 licensed beds. That was less than one per cent of Ontario's long-term-care beds at the time.</p>

      <p>Ontario announced nearly 500 additional beds for Indigenous communities in 2018. The need for culturally appropriate care, however, cannot be measured only by counting beds. It also includes who controls the care, where it is delivered, whether language and culture are part of daily life, and whether Elders remain connected to the people and places they know.</p>

      <p>Staffing is another pressure. The Registered Nurses' Association of Ontario says the province's 626 long-term-care homes are not meeting the needs of approximately 79,000 residents. On July 8, 2026, the Ontario Nurses' Association reported cuts affecting registered nurses, nurse practitioners and other health professionals in hospitals and long-term-care homes across Ontario.</p>

      <p>Those province-wide concerns do not tell us what staffing would look like in the proposed regional home. They are a reason to ask for the staffing, recruitment, retention and cultural-care plan before describing any new building as a complete solution.</p>

      <h2>Seven generations means planning before crisis</h2>

      <p>I was taught to consider three generations behind us, the generation living now and three generations still to come. If a decision cannot be made without harming one of those generations, we should stop and reconsider it.</p>

      <p>For aging policy, that means listening to Elders and families before a plan is fixed. It means investing in people who want to age in the community, not waiting until a health crisis leaves them with no option. It means measuring success through health, belonging, purpose and connection, not only through the number of beds constructed.</p>

      <p>The regional long-term-care proposal may become one part of the answer. Members still need to see the choices, commitments and alternatives clearly. They should be able to compare an off-reserve regional model with community-based care, home supports and examples such as Wiikwemkoong's Elders Home.</p>

      <p>Aging well is not one building. It is the work of creating a community where Elders can remain safe, supported, useful, connected and at home for as long as possible.</p>

